// Irrelevant read introduction -- the b-variant next door is the same program
// with the introduced read commented out.
//
// Source: Sevcik, "Program Transformations in Weak Memory Models" (PhD thesis,
//         Edinburgh 2008). Introducing a read whose value is immediately
//         overwritten is a transformation any model ought to validate; a model
//         whose verdict moves when you add one is broken, and exhibiting that
//         is what the pair is for.
//
// The two files now agree: both forbid r1 = 1 && r2 = 1, and both produce 36
// executions. The outcome is out of thin air either way -- r1 = 1 needs z = 1,
// which needs r2 = 1, which needs y = 1, which in the else branch is y := r1
// again.
//
// This file asserted `allow` until the value-assignment elaboration stopped
// discharging the conjuncts that pin a write's own value. Under `y := r1`
// guarded by `r1 != 0`, the elaboration used to concretise the write to 1 and
// drop the guard in the same step, leaving the write justified by nothing --
// so the introduced read did move the verdict, and this file recorded that it
// had. With the guard retained the read is genuinely irrelevant: 44 executions
// became 36, which is exactly the b-variant's count.
x := 0;
y := 0;
z := 0;
{
  r1 := z;
  if (r1 = 0) {
    r3 := x;
    if (r3 = 1) {
      y := 1
    }
  } else {
    r4 := x;
    r4 := 1;
    y := r1
  }
} ||| {
  x := 1;
  r2 := y;
  z := r2
}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 1) [Sevcik]